LearnFromGames: a fast mode (SetOCRFastMode, off by default) and gray pixels taken as they are.

Fast mode gives the same lines, text and boxes for about half the work on a game's screen:
 - The line recognizers invoke on only the chunks there are, spread over the interpreter pool, where the
   original always runs batches of 8, zero-padded. A dialogue screen is some 20 chunks over three passes (und,
   Jpan, Hani) and was computed as 40.
 - The layout analysis keeps its filters and its recomputed boxes but runs no cluster-sort model, sorts
   nothing, and fills in no colors, line texts or page language: the app orders and groups lines itself.
Lines then come in the order detected, not in reading order. With fast mode off nothing changes.

A kGray_8 bitmap goes to the stages as it is instead of by way of RGBA; the recognizer never read the colors.

